Z||000000||bHooper||08-28-2000||08:58 PM||||OK, so I might of got in a little over my head, tonight.  I decided to install the nice white gauge faces that I got from SXM, last month.<P>First, I broke the lower left support to the instrument panel (i think that can be repaired) , while tearing into everything.  I removed all the gauges, and needle's (being varefull to remember placement), swaped the faceplate's and went to reinstall.<P>Whoops, now all the needle's are off... volt's reading 18+, tach "idle" at 4000rpm, ect.  And these little suckers are hard to put back into place while in the dash!<P>Any good tricks to fix this... besides, not starting this job in the driveway at 7pm on a week night?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P>tia!!! <P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||2
Z||000001||Pontiaddict||08-29-2000||04:56 PM||||You were supposed to remember where they go when the key is on and the gauges are working.(A picture always helps) You also have to replace the needles with the power on to the gauges. With the power on most of the gauges are set to 0<P><BR>If you know how much gas you have, set the gauge to that. If not, then guess and reset it when you find out it's wrong and run out of gas. :0<P>The volt meter can be set by using a handheld voltage meter and seting your gauge to that voltage.<P>Use a thermometer to check the temp, then set the gauge accordingly.<P>That ought to get you back on track.<P>Good luck||||reg||1
